00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:07,842
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is the message you want to convey, so it starts doing on its own?

367
00:33:07,863 --> 00:33:08,883
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it starts to prefer it.

368
00:33:09,023 --> 00:33:10,885
[SPEAKER_03]: To prefer the non-anger over anger.

369
00:33:12,427 --> 00:33:18,730
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the message in relation to anger specifically?

370
00:33:19,290 --> 00:33:20,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you want to be happy in life?

371
00:33:21,951 --> 00:33:22,611
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, yeah.

372
00:33:23,411 --> 00:33:25,332
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're angry, are you happy?

373
00:33:25,352 --> 00:33:25,592
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

374
00:33:26,312 --> 00:33:26,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes?

375
00:33:27,033 --> 00:33:27,673
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

376
00:33:27,693 --> 00:33:29,774
[SPEAKER_03]: No, that's the question you should be asking.

377
00:33:30,554 --> 00:33:32,315
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's the point.

378
00:33:32,335 --> 00:33:38,957
[SPEAKER_03]: The sole reason for the anger is to get back onto the happiness, to get back onto the satisfaction.

379
00:33:41,530 --> 00:33:52,295
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when it gets angry, when it gets disturbed, it doesn't know that it doesn't have to go through the motions of expressing anger and acting out to get where it wants to be from the start.

380
00:33:53,655 --> 00:33:55,836
[SPEAKER_03]: All it needs to do is, oh, I don't want to...

381
00:33:55,856 --> 00:33:59,578
[SPEAKER_03]: I won't be angry, and it's back where you want it to be from the start.

382
00:34:01,405 --> 00:34:10,149
[SPEAKER_02]: The verse from the Dhammapada, aversion cannot be dispelled by aversion, it can only be dispelled by non-aversion.

383
00:34:10,389 --> 00:34:10,609
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

384
00:34:10,949 --> 00:34:14,850
[SPEAKER_03]: But yeah, that's basically in heart of every anger or irritation.

385
00:34:16,271 --> 00:34:25,995
[SPEAKER_03]: It's that pressure from the animal, because it doesn't know that actually, there and then, it has the option to be free from it instantly.

386
00:34:26,795 --> 00:34:29,376
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why anger is quickly dispelled, as the Sutta says.

387
00:34:29,796 --> 00:34:30,397
[SPEAKER_03]: Blameworthy,

388
00:34:31,508 --> 00:34:32,589
[SPEAKER_03]: But quickly dispelled.

389
00:34:33,910 --> 00:34:35,471
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same as sensuality.

390
00:34:35,491 --> 00:34:42,076
[SPEAKER_01]: You're dropping the wanting of this thing, because you don't want it.

391
00:34:43,697 --> 00:34:44,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Where's the pressure there?

392
00:34:45,099 --> 00:34:51,584
[SPEAKER_01]: So, dropping the not-wanting of this pressure, it's irritating pressure.

393
00:34:53,085 --> 00:34:53,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you're no pressure.

394
00:34:53,945 --> 00:34:55,006
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no resistance to it.

395
00:34:55,066 --> 00:34:56,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there's no resistance to it.

396
00:34:57,368 --> 00:34:59,510
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just with anger, you get almost

397
00:35:00,312 --> 00:35:02,773
[SPEAKER_03]: Instantly verified effort.

398
00:35:03,033 --> 00:35:04,113
[SPEAKER_03]: With sensuality, you don't.

399
00:35:05,394 --> 00:35:09,855
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why sensuality is not blameworthy, but a lot harder to give up.

400
00:35:12,096 --> 00:35:13,117
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what they describe.

401
00:35:14,697 --> 00:35:20,379
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in a sense of, like, say somebody makes you angry, somebody insults you.

402
00:35:21,119 --> 00:35:22,080
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, they don't make you angry.

403
00:35:22,100 --> 00:35:22,780
[SPEAKER_03]: They insult you.

404
00:35:23,280 --> 00:35:24,160
[SPEAKER_03]: They do something to you.

405
00:35:24,180 --> 00:35:25,341
[SPEAKER_03]: You get angry on account of it.

406
00:35:26,321 --> 00:35:28,322
[SPEAKER_03]: So, somebody disrespects you.

407
00:35:29,935 --> 00:35:33,337
[SPEAKER_03]: And now you have all these options of how you would deal with that.

408
00:35:35,158 --> 00:35:44,844
[SPEAKER_03]: They would obviously require that person, or even more people, and so on, and it's this and that to address, it never happens again, so it's corrected, so they know their mistake, all of that.

409
00:35:45,745 --> 00:35:50,668
[SPEAKER_03]: But obviously, that would be exactly on the level of, okay, so the anger, you believe the anger came from over there.

410
00:35:51,528 --> 00:35:59,093
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's how you're going to treat this condition, by talking about it, and sharing your feelings, and making everything known, how you feel inside, to everybody.

411
00:35:59,815 --> 00:36:01,557
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that will work only for how long?

412
00:36:01,757 --> 00:36:02,517
[SPEAKER_03]: Till somebody forgets.

413
00:36:03,478 --> 00:36:08,803
[SPEAKER_03]: Or until you are in a bad mood and then start interpreting everything as disrespect, because of your own insecurity.

414
00:36:10,325 --> 00:36:12,907
[SPEAKER_03]: But instead, say somebody intentionally disrespects you.

415
00:36:13,027 --> 00:36:13,988
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not even accidental.

416
00:36:15,469 --> 00:36:26,179
[SPEAKER_03]: And as I said, you have an option to take on now that burden of dealing with this, or to say, okay, I do not have to deal with this person ever again for the rest of my life.

417
00:36:27,648 --> 00:36:28,948
[SPEAKER_03]: And isn't that a wonderful feeling?

418
00:36:29,769 --> 00:36:32,009
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, completely elevated.

419
00:36:32,089 --> 00:36:34,970
[SPEAKER_03]: Any burden, any sense, no hatred, no love either.

420
00:36:36,011 --> 00:36:40,992
[SPEAKER_03]: But certainly, there is no reason for hatred, because there is nothing I'm trying to express or deal with.

421
00:36:41,972 --> 00:36:43,533
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, you're not my problem anymore.

422
00:36:43,673 --> 00:36:44,033
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you.

423
00:36:44,913 --> 00:36:45,934
[SPEAKER_03]: Actually, I like you for that.

424
00:36:46,894 --> 00:36:50,455
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, I have no sense of duty towards you, because you didn't earn it.

425
00:36:52,744 --> 00:36:54,244
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the same when the Buddha went to the...

426
00:36:54,264 --> 00:36:54,545
[SPEAKER_03]: Gift.

427
00:36:55,765 --> 00:36:57,445
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the gift, yeah, yeah, exactly.

428
00:36:57,485 --> 00:36:59,506
[SPEAKER_03]: But no, I meant the Kosambi, when they were quarreling.

429
00:37:00,186 --> 00:37:03,987
[SPEAKER_03]: When he said, stop quarreling, it's, you know, you're incurring a lot of demerit.

430
00:37:04,027 --> 00:37:06,028
[SPEAKER_03]: And he says, well, mind your own business, basically.

431
00:37:06,468 --> 00:37:08,149
[SPEAKER_03]: It's our demerit, not your demerit.

432
00:37:09,089 --> 00:37:11,230
[SPEAKER_03]: And he told them three times, and three times they dismissed him.

433
00:37:11,250 --> 00:37:13,650
[SPEAKER_03]: He's like, okay, this foolish man, and he went off.

434
00:37:13,670 --> 00:37:18,432
[SPEAKER_03]: And you think, like, oh, so he was completely disrespected.

435
00:37:19,852 --> 00:37:20,052
[SPEAKER_03]: And what

436
00:37:23,933 --> 00:37:24,854
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, not my problem.

437
00:37:25,594 --> 00:37:32,419
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, when Sariputta and Mahatma Gandhi came back and started, come on, let's let's go back and help them, he did.

438
00:37:33,419 --> 00:37:35,381
[SPEAKER_03]: He wasn't angry with them.

439
00:37:35,621 --> 00:37:38,242
[SPEAKER_03]: No, well, now I won't go, because they offended me.

440
00:37:39,944 --> 00:37:42,906
[SPEAKER_03]: No, they said, oh, you know, they were obviously lost perspective.

441
00:37:42,946 --> 00:37:43,806
[SPEAKER_03]: It's for their welfare.

442
00:37:44,326 --> 00:37:44,747
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's do it.

443
00:37:44,787 --> 00:37:45,507
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, let's do it.

444
00:37:46,708 --> 00:37:47,028
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

445
00:37:47,048 --> 00:37:52,332
[SPEAKER_03]: But either way, basically, by people, people, by disrespect, by

446
00:37:53,922 --> 00:38:03,289
[SPEAKER_03]: Any, any kind of abuse directed at you, that in itself automatically reminds you how free you are from them.

447
00:38:06,011 --> 00:38:07,493
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the gift-suitor.

448
00:38:08,133 --> 00:38:11,896
[SPEAKER_03]: When he's throwing insults at him and says, oh, let me give you an example.

449
00:38:11,936 --> 00:38:15,198
[SPEAKER_03]: If you give gifts and they don't accept it, who did... like, I can't accept it.

450
00:38:15,339 --> 00:38:16,580
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I don't know, I can't.

451
00:38:16,820 --> 00:38:18,521
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm unable to accept what you're giving me.

452
00:38:19,342 --> 00:38:20,402
[SPEAKER_03]: Who does that stay with?

453
00:38:20,643 --> 00:38:20,983
[SPEAKER_03]: With you.

454
00:38:22,358 --> 00:38:25,659
[SPEAKER_03]: That entire experience stays with you, does not become my problem.

455
00:38:26,339 --> 00:38:38,063
[SPEAKER_03]: All because citta has been taught to recognize that very thing which didn't recognize before, which is why the whole pressure and then you couldn't stop thinking about it and winding yourself up more and more and more and more.

456
00:38:39,884 --> 00:38:40,884
[SPEAKER_00]: No one can make you angry.

457
00:38:41,344 --> 00:38:42,124
[SPEAKER_03]: No one can make you angry.

458
00:38:43,905 --> 00:38:47,146
[SPEAKER_03]: And people trying to make you angry would then make you even less angry.

459
00:38:48,726 --> 00:38:49,547
[SPEAKER_03]: More unangry.

460
00:38:52,696 --> 00:38:54,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Because nobody ever could make you angry.

461
00:38:54,297 --> 00:38:56,038
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no, of course, yeah.

462
00:38:58,059 --> 00:39:18,088
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there's a pressure towards anger, you realize, okay, not expressing it, not giving into it, not acting out of it, and then conveying the message to the chitta that, actually, just try it, just, just, you know, humor me for a second, drop it, and you'll see that's what you wanted from the start, when you wanted to go through the anger, every time you did in the past.

463
00:39:21,885 --> 00:39:23,785
[SPEAKER_03]: And actually, it's the same for sensuality as well.

464
00:39:25,366 --> 00:39:31,527
[SPEAKER_03]: The outcome of it is what you want and you can actually have more direct access to it by dropping sensuality.

465
00:39:32,747 --> 00:39:41,729
[SPEAKER_03]: That's that the monk who said to the deva, when he said like, oh, why are you forsaking what's visible here and now for some uncertain goal later?

466
00:39:41,749 --> 00:39:42,910
[SPEAKER_03]: He's like, no, what you're talking about?

467
00:39:42,970 --> 00:39:43,730
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the other way around.

468
00:39:44,770 --> 00:39:47,051
[SPEAKER_03]: I am practicing what's accessible here and now.

469
00:39:47,571 --> 00:39:48,071
[SPEAKER_03]: Instead,

470
00:39:48,861 --> 00:39:57,330
[SPEAKER_03]: of the uncertain results later, which is sensuality, is always uncertain, implies the time, implies the sequence, implies the uncertainty.

471
00:39:57,371 --> 00:40:03,978
[SPEAKER_03]: Giving it up immediately, then and then, gives you the satisfaction you wanted through it.

